    {
      "block_id": "2605.25036_p3_b4",
      "text": "In summary, our contributions are threefold:\n",
      "bbox": [
        55.44,
        242.48,
        231.68,
        252.44
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        221,
        969,
        926,
        1009
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 9.96,
      "font_name": "NimbusRomNo9L-Regu",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 44,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.25036_p3_b5",
      "text": "\u2022 We present a novel perspective on modality misalignment\nin LVLMs. Through rigorous quantitative analysis, we un-\ncover the phenomenon of language bias, where LVLMs\nneglect visual information and over-rely on internal lan-\nguage priors during multimodal training.\n\u2022 We introduce Language Bias Regularization (LBR) and\nLanguage Bias Penalty (LBP), two versatile and eas-\nily deployable methods that require neither additional\nmodels nor external data. Each method can be seam-\nlessly integrated into its respective stage\u2014LBR into vi-\nsual instruction tuning and LBP into DPO training\u2014to\neffectively mitigate language bias.\n\u2022 We conduct extensive evaluations across models of vary-\ning scales and a wide range of datasets, demonstrating\nthe effectiveness and strong generalization capability of\nLBR and LBP. The results confirm that our methods sig-\nnificantly improve vision-language alignment in LVLMs,\nwhile also providing empirical validation for our analysis\nof language bias.\n",
      "bbox": [
        56.93,
        260.41,
        291.1,
        485.57
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        227,
        1041,
        1164,
        1942
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 9.96,
      "font_name": "NimbusRomNo9L-Regu",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 980,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.25036_p3_b6",
      "text": "2. Related Work\n",
      "bbox": [
        55.44,
        502.55,
        138.55,
        514.51
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        221,
        2010,
        554,
        2058
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 11.96,
      "font_name": "NimbusRomNo9L-Medi",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 15,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.25036_p3_b7",
      "text": "Language Bias and Hallucination in LVLMs. Halluci-\nnation (Sun et al., 2024; Zhou et al., 2024; Huang et al.,\n2024a; Bai et al., 2024), a phenomenon where the model\ngenerates linguistically fluent yet visually inconsistent de-\nscriptions of image content, stands as a critical challenge\nin modern LVLMs. This issue is widely attributed to lan-\nguage bias (Jiang et al., 2025): the tendency of models to\nprioritize linguistic patterns over visual faithfulness. While\nnumerous mitigation strategies have been proposed, span-\nning instruction fine-tuning (Liu et al., 2024a; Jiang et al.,\n2024a), preference learning (Yu et al., 2024b;c; Yang et al.,\n2025), and improved decoding methods (Leng et al., 2024;\nLi et al., 2025), the underlying mechanisms of language bias\nremain poorly understood. Specifically, current understand-\ning is predominantly qualitative; the field lacks a formal\ndefinition and the rigorous quantitative analysis required to\n",
      "bbox": [
        55.44,
        523.29,
        291.1,
        712.83
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        221,
        2093,
        1164,
        2851
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 10.06,
      "font_name": "NimbusRomNo9L-Medi",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 946,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.25036_p3_b8",
      "text": "address the problem at its core.\n",
      "bbox": [
        307.44,
        242.48,
        431.39,
        252.44
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        1229,
